WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:06.800
- Good afternoon.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.960
Thank you for your patience,

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.200
for waiting patiently as
we made our way up here.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.120
My name is Eddie Mandhry,

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.870
Director for Africa and the
Middle East at Yale University.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:21.140
And Your Excellency, honorable ministers,

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.560
permanent secretaries, (indistinct),

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.520
members of the delegation,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.453
I extend a heartfelt Yale
University welcome today.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.400
We're absolutely delighted
to have you join us,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.110
for this African Leaders Forum lecture.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.990
Each year, on the sidelines

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:44.650
of the United Nations General Assembly.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:49.650
We have a meeting, or convening
of Heads of State at Yale,

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.442
so that they can deliver,

00:00:52.442 --> 00:00:54.210
some kind of vision for their nation,

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.210
and their transformation and
development of their economies.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:00.250
This will be the 7th such lecture at Yale.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:04.190
And first following the start
of the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:05.480
This lecture is presented

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.510
as part of the Yale Africa Initiative,

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:11.340
a university-wide effort,
launched by President Salovey,

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.210
at his inauguration in 2013.

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:16.950
Aimed at refocusing our
engagements across Africa,

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.520
elevating discourse on Africa at Yale,

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.780
attracting some of the
best and brightest students

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.470
from across the continent,

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.390
among them current
Botswana's cabinet member,

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.635
Kefentse Mzwilina.

00:01:28.635 --> 00:01:32.920
And one other critical
dimension of the initiative,

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.410
is our efforts to foster
bi-directional collaboration

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.200
in areas of mutual interest,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.590
with partners across the continent.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.580
President Masisi's visit to Yale,

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.100
is particularly significant
because moments ago,

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.830
His Excellency signed a
memorandum of understanding,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.330
between Yale and the
Government of Botswana,

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:52.130
for new partnership that
will serve as a launchpad,

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:55.500
for mutually beneficial
exchanges and cooperation

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.930
between both sides on
research and training,

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:02.220
focused on various aspects of
natural resource management.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.300
His Excellency Mokwgweetsi
Masisi will speak on his vision

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.290
for transitioning Botswana
to a knowledge-based economy.

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:10.480
His lecture will be followed by Q&amp;A,

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.480
moderated Q&amp;A conversation here with me,

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.160
and an opportunity for him
to respond to questions,

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.810
from some of you in the audience.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.890
Please mute your mobile devices.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.080
And before His Excellence, it comes up,

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.500
it's my pleasure to invite to the podium,

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.410
a member of our own community,

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.970
a PhD candidate at Yale
School of the Environment,

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.200
who has spent a fair
amount of time in Botswana.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.160
Working with our
counterparts at local NGOs,

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.975
and the Okavango Research Institute

00:02:39.975 --> 00:02:40.900
at the University of Botswana.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.400
She's passionate about,

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.210
the nexus between livestock and wildlife,

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:49.090
and how humans, livestock, and
wildlife impact each other.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:50.650
Most recently, she worked

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:54.090
in the Boteti West
sub-district in Botswana.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.610
Focused on the interactions between

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:57.750
the Central Kalahari Game Reserve,

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:01.930
and the Makadikadi National Park. (laughs)

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.506
I hope I got that right?

00:03:03.506 --> 00:03:04.339
- [President Masisi] Close.

00:03:04.339 --> 00:03:05.460
- Close. (laughs)

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.832
Kaggie Orrick, I welcome
you to this stage.

00:03:09.832 --> 00:03:12.249
(applauding)

00:03:16.411 --> 00:03:21.278
- (speaking in foreign language)

00:03:21.278 --> 00:03:23.695
(applauding)

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:28.627
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:32.801
- [President Masisi] Oh, thank you.

00:03:32.801 --> 00:03:36.834
- Very honored to be welcoming
His Excellency to Yale.

00:03:36.834 --> 00:03:40.130
His Excellency, President
Mokgweetsi Masisi,

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:43.210
began his career in the education sector.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.560
From 1989 to 2004,

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:48.760
he had a variety of
positions in public sector,

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.400
including national coordinator

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.940
for Social Studies Education in Botswana.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.440
Education officer in the United Nations,

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.780
and HIV/AIDS, prevention researcher

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.590
with the Tropical Disease Research Center.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:06.030
In the 2000s, politics came
knocking, and heated the call.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.150
In 2009, he successfully
contested the election

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.420
to serve as a member of
parliament for Moshupa.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.440
Subsequently, he held various
cabinet level appointments

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:16.830
from public administration,

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:19.540
to education and skills development.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:23.860
In 2014, he was endorsed as
the vice president of Botswana,

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.740
and was given responsibility
overseeing national programs

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.337
spanning from poverty eradication,

00:04:29.337 --> 00:04:33.310
economic diversification,
and job creation.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.050
In July of 2015,

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.110
President Masisi became the chairperson

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.450
of the ruling Botswana Democratic Party,

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.230
and retain that post through 2017.

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:44.770
In 2018, President Masisi was elected

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.490
as the fifth president of Botswana.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.980
In the October of 2019 President
Masisi was reappointed.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.780
And as for a five-year term,

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.550
after the Botswana Democratic Party

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:56.110
won the general elections,

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:59.190
by garnering the majority
in the national assembly.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:02.260
President Masisi holds
a master's of science

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:03.790
in social studies education

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:05.600
and instructional systems designed

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.350
from Florida State University.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:11.130
And a graduate degree in
economics and and social policy

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.570
from Manchester University,

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.610
where he was a distinguished
achieving scholar.

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:18.340
It is my absolute distinct
pleasure and honor

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.080
to be welcoming His Excellency,
President Mokgweetsi Masisi,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.140
to the podium,

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:26.210
to deliver his Yale African
Leaders Forum lecture,

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.870
entitled, "Botswana's Vision
for a Knowledge-Based Economy."

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:32.577
Thank you. (applauding)

00:05:41.755 --> 00:05:43.460
- Thank you.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:44.460
Thank you very much.

00:05:46.691 --> 00:05:50.441
(speaking foreign language).

00:05:56.670 --> 00:06:01.670
President Peter Salovey, in absentia,

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:03.763
President of Yale University,

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:08.683
distinguished members of the faculty,

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:15.030
the Director for Africa
Initiative, the Yale community,

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:20.923
my distinguished fellow
Motswana, from Boteti West.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.990
Honorable Minister Kwape,

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:27.700
and senior government
officials from Botswana,

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.143
ladies and gentlemen, a very good evening.

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:33.873
It is a distinct honor,

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:38.300
for me to have been
invited to this occasion,

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.970
to address you, on an important subject,

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:44.540
regarding Botswana's perspective,

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.193
on a knowledge-based economy.

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:52.273
This honor, does not
only extend to myself,

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:57.873
but it also extends to Botswana,
the people of Botswana.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:03.270
My acceptance of the invitation
from the Yale community

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:07.160
is a living testament, to
this special relationship,

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:10.300
that has been established
and continues to flourish,

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:13.683
between the Republic of
Botswana and Yale University.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:19.560
I suppose, a living testimony
of the Yale Africa Initiative.

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:27.190
Yale University and its living
commitment to excellence,

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:30.693
remains a source of
inspiration to all of us,

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.760
who hold similar dreams to a path,

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.623
to a sustainable and prosperous future.

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:43.120
I have no doubt, that the
seeds of collaboration,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:46.773
that we have already
sown, will fully blossom,

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.380
for the mutual benefit of our friendship.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:55.440
It is in this regard, that
I wish to share with you,

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.003
where Botswana is headed,

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:02.183
as we March towards a
knowledge-based economy.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:06.363
Such an ambitious and noble initiative,

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:11.870
is also an invitation
to the Yale community,

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:16.233
to join us, and help us, in this journey.

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:22.773
At the independence of Botswana in 1966,

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:26.460
Botswana was one of the
poorest countries in the world.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:29.490
Despite a small population,

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:34.170
subsistence agriculture was
the main source of livelihood,

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:36.403
for the people of Botswana.

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:40.240
There was some observers,

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.250
who even questioned the state of our mind,

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:46.743
for taking the bold step
of seeking independence.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:52.580
However, with innate determination,

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:54.723
our forefathers were not deterred,

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:58.360
from going ahead with the project.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.810
The country dependent
heavily on foreign aid

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:04.023
for both its recurrent
and development budget.

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:09.140
However, Botswana's
economic fortunes changed,

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:13.743
in the early 1970s, following
the discovery of diamonds,

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:19.723
which became the main state
of our economy to this day.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:24.010
Our founding fathers who
managed our resources,

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:26.460
with a high degree of frugality,

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.760
invested heavily in the construction

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:33.760
of schools, roads, hospitals, airports,

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:38.290
as well as the provision
of basic social services,

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:40.720
which has taken the form of free,

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:44.060
and affordable education amongst others,

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:46.573
to enhance a better standard of living.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.550
Botswana's natural
resources have also been,

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:55.560
in the form of our natural
endowments, namely,

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:00.560
mineral deposits, flora and
fauna, the land, and the people.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:05.210
The primary focus, in regard to these,

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:08.340
has been the people, as the owners,

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:11.320
beneficiaries, and the driving force,

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:15.673
behind the expectation of such resources.

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:21.020
The country graduated
to high middle income,

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:26.020
but as the economy grew,
the population multiplied

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.663
at a faster pace than
economic development.

00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:33.280
Even though, we have invested
a lot in human capital,

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:36.260
the productive capacity has decreased,

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:40.530
and we need to re-tool
and re-skill our people,

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:42.520
in order to make them relevant,

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.023
to the dictates of the global markets.

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:48.113
In our national vision,

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:51.430
we have set ourselves amongst others,

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:56.293
the target of being a high
income country by the year 2036.

00:10:57.320 --> 00:11:00.560
The vision further in joins us to strive,

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:02.663
to become a knowledge-based economy.

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:08.210
Distinguished guests,
knowledge and technology,

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:11.813
are in the heart of economic
development for any country.

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:16.460
And knowledge economy,
as we may all be aware,

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:19.470
is largely driven by three main factors,

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:24.270
being human capital, relevant
and effective policies,

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:26.510
and the requisite information

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:29.470
and communication
technology infrastructure.

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:32.150
Essentially, a knowledge economy,

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:36.540
is realized when most income
and economic activity,

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.970
is driven by content and knowledge,

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:42.370
rather than other physical resources.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:46.090
At best, such content and knowledge

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:51.090
should be domestically
contrived and patented.

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:57.550
As a country, we have prioritized

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:02.550
in our current national
development plan for policies,

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:05.280
that will drive economic development,

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.793
towards attainment of
sustainable economic development.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:13.493
These are; promotion of export led growth,

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:18.410
ensuring more efficient
government spending and financing,

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:19.700
building human capital,

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:22.803
and the provision of
appropriate infrastructure.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:27.140
The tourism sector, has
been a long time accounted

00:12:28.242 --> 00:12:31.030
for the majority of Botswana services.

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:36.030
Though, others exist such
as financial services,

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:40.620
administration and
other business services.

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:42.780
Information technology services,

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.920
and the range of professional services.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:48.600
Many of these require knowledge-based...

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:52.890
Many of these require
knowledge-based specialized skills,

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:56.760
and for us as a country, we
need to support the development

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:00.453
by strengthening intellectual
property framework.

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:03.850
Ladies and gentlemen,

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:07.460
getting the educational basics is a right,

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:10.400
is a prerequisite, for equipping Botswana

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:13.060
with the skills to
successfully transition,

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:14.523
to a knowledge-based economy.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:19.770
Educating our people and
sustaining productive human capital

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:23.370
has always been at the core
of our economic development

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:24.993
since independence.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:29.130
Some of the most specialized skills,

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:31.450
that we need to create and manage,

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:35.270
the fourth industrial
revolution, FIR technologies,

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:40.270
such as coding, genetics,
data science and analytics,

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:42.330
well in due course,

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:46.793
needs to be central to school
and college curriculum.

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:51.005
This is why this financial year,

00:13:51.005 --> 00:13:53.380
the government has committed about 24%

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:55.983
of the overall national
budget to education.

00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:01.850
This translates to 15.730 billion Pula,

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:06.980
or about 1,000,000,573 U.S. dollars,

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:10.863
making it the largest share
in our national budget.

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:15.350
The proposed budget will
cater for the development

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:18.580
and sourcing of e-content for schools,

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:23.550
training teachers, and school
managers in basic ICT skills,

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:26.100
and the use of technology
on teaching and learning.

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:30.000
The allocation is geared towards providing

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.060
appropriate education and training,

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:36.780
to students, so that upon
completion of their studies,

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:40.470
they could become active
players in the transition

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:43.423
of the country to a
knowledge-based economy.

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:48.060
This will include
improving the foundations

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:50.790
of learning and the
quality of our education,

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:54.800
and the scope of pre-primary education.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:56.500
At the secondary level,

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:59.370
there will be focused on
improving outcomes for learners

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:02.330
in terms of maths and science.

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:06.473
Areas where performance has
been below expectations.

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:11.280
Furthermore, the speed
of change in technology

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:14.080
and production, obligates

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:16.780
that learning be a lifelong process.

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:20.320
And that today's learners will
need to have the capacity,

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:22.850
to really equip themselves with skills,

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:26.750
perhaps several times, during
their working lifetimes.

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:29.550
Hence, the importance of softer skills,

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:32.133
such as flexibility and initiative.

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:36.420
This will be important,
not just for individuals,

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:39.040
but also for the economy as a whole.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:43.410
One of the challenges of FIR,
is that emerging technologies

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:46.700
such as automation,
artificial intelligence,

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:51.190
genetic engineering, and
blockchain, may displace jobs,

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:54.020
as production becomes
more technology intensive

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:55.483
and capital intensive.

00:15:56.820 --> 00:15:59.190
Distinguished guests in
view of the global trends

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:01.830
towards a knowledge economy,

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:05.190
we have embarked on an ambitious project,

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:08.830
to broaden the national digital ecosystem,

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:11.930
through the national digital
transformation strategy,

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:14.560
commonly known as SmartSpot,

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:18.903
to deliver a smart, sustainable
society for our country.

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:24.270
The strategy, aims to
transform the public sector,

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:29.270
to efficiently provide services,
to citizens and businesses.

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:34.560
The SmartSpot strategy also
seeks to increase mobile network

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:36.720
and mobile broadband coverage.

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:39.610
And to provide internet connectivity

00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:41.270
to government facilities,

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:44.200
and strategic locations
across the country,

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:45.810
have been identified

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:49.603
for connection to ensure the most impact.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:53.770
As reflected in my
government's reset agenda,

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:55.480
which has prioritized five areas

00:16:56.416 --> 00:16:57.390
in our development trajectory.

00:16:57.390 --> 00:16:59.960
Digitization has been identified,

00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:03.370
as one of the priority areas
that has the immense potential

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:07.150
to unlock and enable high
productivity among our people,

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:09.603
the majority of whom are youth.

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:13.373
Trending knowledge-based services,

00:17:14.600 --> 00:17:15.830
require upgraded connectivity,

00:17:15.830 --> 00:17:20.460
with high quality, dependable,
low cost bandwidth,

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:22.380
stimulated through the widespread rollers

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:24.460
of e-government services,

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:26.900
and ensuring effective operation,

00:17:26.900 --> 00:17:28.830
of the national ICT broadband,

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:30.913
and lower local area networks.

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:32.743
To this end,

00:17:32.743 --> 00:17:36.100
part of the second largest
share of by development budgets

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:37.900
will cater for information, communication,

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:39.370
technology projects,

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:42.440
being the government
data network upgrading,

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:45.970
national broadband networks,
government data center,

00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:48.333
and local access network.

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:51.260
My government continues to look

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.420
for strategic development partners,

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:56.630
who can assist the country to capitalize,

00:17:56.630 --> 00:17:59.930
on the fourth industrial
revolution technologies,

00:17:59.930 --> 00:18:03.230
to advance our objective
of connecting our people

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:05.113
for the betterment of their lives.

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:10.000
I want to highlight the fact
that Botswana subscribes

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.240
to an open market economy,

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:16.283
and government's role is
only facilitative in nature.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.290
Therefore, I want to assure you

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:22.620
that we remain committed to governance,

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:24.180
that facilitates the private sector

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:27.300
as an engine of economic growth.

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:28.990
The creator of wealth,

00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:32.623
and the much needed employment
opportunities for our people.

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:38.310
This in a nutshell, is
a synopsis of our vision

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:40.610
for a transformative process,

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:43.000
towards a knowledge-based economy.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:46.870
I will once again, enjoying
the Yale community,

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:48.420
and other willing partners,

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:51.810
to accompany us on the promising journey.

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:53.360
I thank you for your attention.

00:18:54.666 --> 00:18:57.083
(applauding)

00:19:00.268 --> 00:19:01.101
- Your Excellency kindly take a seat.

00:19:01.101 --> 00:19:02.530
- Yeah.

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:03.363
- This one.

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:16.260
- Thank you very much, Mr. President,

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:20.170
for really engaging lecture,
centered around your vision

00:19:21.017 --> 00:19:23.820
for knowledge-based economy in Botswana.

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:26.678
I'd like to begin with a small anecdote.

00:19:26.678 --> 00:19:29.840
I heard that you earlier had
an engagement with Bloomberg.

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:34.274
And in 1965, there was a film.

00:19:34.274 --> 00:19:37.230
There's a book published by Ian Fleming,

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:39.040
now widely read and even adapted

00:19:40.238 --> 00:19:42.000
to a movie in 1971 James Bond.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:45.020
Film by the same names,
"Diamonds Are Forever."

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:47.047
Mr. President rumor has it.

00:19:47.047 --> 00:19:47.880
You're in New York,

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:48.713
in the possession of the
world's third largest diamond,

00:19:48.713 --> 00:19:50.796
(laughs)

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:55.903
one found earlier this year,

00:19:55.903 --> 00:20:00.080
1,175 carrots at a mine
Karowe in Botswana,

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:01.280
is this true?

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:03.550
And if so, can you
officially put the debate

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:05.323
to rest, are diamonds forever?

00:20:06.578 --> 00:20:08.661
(laughs)

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:13.183
- You know, thanks for the question.

00:20:14.070 --> 00:20:15.483
Let me begin with the facts.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:20.060
Has the diamond been discovered

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:22.313
that's the third largest in the world?

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:24.143
True.

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:28.273
Was it discovered at Karowe Mine,

00:20:28.273 --> 00:20:33.273
a Karowe Mine owned by Lucara
Diamond, a Canadian company?

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:35.193
True.

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:39.080
Was it in Boteti West? (laughs)

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:39.913
True.

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:45.920
Is it about 1,175 carats?

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:46.753
True.

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:48.733
Is it rough?

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:51.330
True.

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:53.150
Is it a diamond?

00:20:53.150 --> 00:20:55.503
It's a diamond. (laughs)

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:58.900
Were we in possession of it this morning,

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:00.480
or holding it on launching?

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:02.883
True. (laughing)

00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:04.780
Do we own it? (laughs)

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:05.613
No.

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:09.817
It is owned by Lucara,

00:21:09.817 --> 00:21:12.300
and I'm really proud to introduce somebody

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:14.790
who is very close associated with it.

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:17.330
One of the world's leading lights,

00:21:17.330 --> 00:21:19.950
in the diamond trade and industry,

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:22.960
and technology and management Oded.

00:21:24.893 --> 00:21:25.726
- Oh, Oded.

00:21:25.726 --> 00:21:27.883
- Welcome. (applauding)

00:21:28.946 --> 00:21:30.410
From HB Antwerp.

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:33.096
He's one of the partners with Lucara

00:21:33.096 --> 00:21:35.930
who are going to be managing that diamond.

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:36.763
- Yes.

00:21:36.763 --> 00:21:39.730
- And doing what they think is best

00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:42.393
to give it additional value.

00:21:43.270 --> 00:21:45.030
And what are we waiting for is government.

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:47.600
We want them to succeed the most,

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:49.860
get the most money out of it,

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:54.420
because then we just
get some tax. (laughs)

00:21:54.420 --> 00:21:56.502
We get royalty.
- Yeah.

00:21:56.502 --> 00:22:01.502
- And we urge them to
go out and find more,

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.290
because they're going underground.

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:05.770
- All right,

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:08.134
well, you haven't--
- And yes,

00:22:08.134 --> 00:22:09.578
diamonds are forever.(laughs)

00:22:09.578 --> 00:22:11.620
- Okay, well you have
settled the debate to then.

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:15.510
Well, thank you for outlining
kind of all the dimensions

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:19.040
of this Karowe diamond that was founded.

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:20.550
I mean, so we convened

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:25.010
in an interesting time of
unprecedented disruption

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:27.457
in our global community.

00:22:27.457 --> 00:22:32.457
The pandemic has caused a
lot of pain, anguish, death,

00:22:32.670 --> 00:22:36.263
loss of life, and economic
struggle across the world.

00:22:37.372 --> 00:22:38.330
How are the people of Botswana doing?

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:39.530
And how are you fairing?

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:48.370
- The COVID-19 pandemic
has been devastating.

00:22:49.433 --> 00:22:52.130
It has had a deleterious
effect on our economy,

00:22:52.130 --> 00:22:54.023
on our livelihoods and people.

00:22:56.733 --> 00:22:58.603
But we are resilient people,

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:04.440
and so we've got to face
up to it, and confront it.

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:06.460
And the best confrontation,

00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:09.620
when you're a third
world developing country,

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:13.320
without the power of the by chemical,

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:18.320
or by medical response,
is to use people power.

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:22.880
And use your intellect to manage it,

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:26.380
on the base of the advice
we get from scientists.

00:23:26.380 --> 00:23:29.060
And I'm glad this country, though,

00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:30.730
our university has given us

00:23:30.730 --> 00:23:32.543
some of the best brains to do that.

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:36.810
Who is here with us today,
Professor Mosepele Mosepele,

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:38.020
trained in Harvard.
- Oh.

00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:42.000
- He is our deputy coordinator
for COVID-19, (clapping)

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:44.400
and infectious disease specialist.

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:47.180
And so they us on what to do,

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:48.820
and we follow that advice.

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:51.090
And we regulate on that advice.

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:53.230
We communicate on that advice.

00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:56.820
He's on national TV every
week is a household name.

00:23:56.820 --> 00:24:01.300
And he basically dispenses,
this advice in language

00:24:01.300 --> 00:24:04.800
that people understand, and can relate to,

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:09.800
and he practices medicine,
and teachers, young doctors

00:24:10.889 --> 00:24:13.090
at the university teaching hospital.

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:16.580
And so, we have responded
in ways that we can best,

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:17.790
but it's affected us.

00:24:17.790 --> 00:24:21.960
We've had a loss of life, we loss of jobs,

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:24.513
loss of opportunity, loss of market.

00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:30.360
And we've seen our bank balances,

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:33.520
and debit accounts depleted,

00:24:33.520 --> 00:24:34.993
as we buy the necessary PPE

00:24:36.102 --> 00:24:39.490
and other interventions, such as vaccines.

00:24:39.490 --> 00:24:41.700
Even though, we paid so many vaccines

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:43.977
that have not yet arrived yet.

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:47.351
- Thank you for outlining that.

00:24:47.351 --> 00:24:49.930
And we also would like to note that,

00:24:49.930 --> 00:24:54.239
we do welcome people who've
been to the university up north

00:24:54.239 --> 00:24:56.480
that won't be named as part
of our community. (laughs)

00:24:56.480 --> 00:24:59.960
Not much Crimson around,
but that's okay. (laughs)

00:24:59.960 --> 00:25:01.820
But we appreciate how difficult

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:03.360
and challenging this time must be,

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:05.590
for you and the people of Botswana.

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:09.780
And we hope that as vaccines
become more accessible,

00:25:09.780 --> 00:25:12.423
and that there is some light
at the end of the tunnel.

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:15.170
Many leaders, some leaders

00:25:15.170 --> 00:25:17.610
who have come to speak
at Yale in the past,

00:25:17.610 --> 00:25:22.123
have sometimes pointed to
the miracle of Singapore.

00:25:23.290 --> 00:25:26.310
Lee Kuan Yew model of
transformation and development

00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:30.780
as being one of the pathways
they aspire to taking,

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:32.650
to transform our continent.

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:35.030
I'm curious about your sentiments

00:25:36.151 --> 00:25:37.500
on several African countries,

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:41.390
steering to look eastwards to partners,

00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:43.820
and for opportunities
for trade and commerce,

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:46.180
technology transfer and so on.

00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:48.730
China obviously, comes to mind as well.

00:25:48.730 --> 00:25:50.010
So I'd be curious about

00:25:50.010 --> 00:25:51.980
your international engagement strategy,

00:25:51.980 --> 00:25:54.243
and your engagement in the East.

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:58.853
- Well, broadly speaking, we
have very open foreign policy.

00:25:59.690 --> 00:26:00.863
We are non-aligned,

00:26:02.340 --> 00:26:04.950
because it's in our best
interest not to be aligned.

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:07.110
It's in our best interest,

00:26:07.110 --> 00:26:08.760
not to be dealing with problems

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:11.310
that are not going to help us develop.

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:14.980
And so, what's on this
foreign policy is premised,

00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:19.980
on protecting and growing
Botswana's interests first.

00:26:20.320 --> 00:26:24.283
We have friends, both in
the East and the West,

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:28.283
from whom we derive a lot of benefits.

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:32.750
We exchange views and we share values,

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:34.940
but we do have differences.

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:38.030
And those differences we
manage diplomatically,

00:26:38.030 --> 00:26:42.690
but, those things are
essential for our development,

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:45.430
and growth, we negotiate for,

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:49.720
and we take what's best
for us at the time.

00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:51.773
And both have a lot to offer.

00:26:53.060 --> 00:26:56.745
So, we're in the lucky
position of shopping,(laughs)

00:26:56.745 --> 00:26:59.080
and making you compete.

00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:02.520
And we pick what we think
is the most competitive.

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:03.923
We're not always right.

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:08.480
And so we learned from those experiences.

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:10.720
So we do shop in the West,

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:13.007
we've shopped in the West much more

00:27:13.007 --> 00:27:13.910
than we shopped in the East,

00:27:13.910 --> 00:27:18.860
but we also do shop in the East,
for both ideas and content.

00:27:18.860 --> 00:27:23.860
And so, it's a (indistinct) to say that,

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:29.070
we do work very harmoniously with China,

00:27:29.260 --> 00:27:33.530
very harmoniously with the
United States and others.

00:27:33.530 --> 00:27:36.120
As for the model of development,

00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:39.673
yes, Singapore is done exceedingly well,

00:27:42.026 --> 00:27:45.130
but, Singapore is not Botswana,
and Botswana is Singapore.

00:27:45.130 --> 00:27:47.790
For one, the land mass is different,

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:50.800
their weather patterns are different.

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:53.693
We in Botswana have a big budget,

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:58.410
that is dedicated to
a wildlife department.

00:27:58.410 --> 00:28:02.210
Something Singapore
could never have, right.

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:05.823
In Botswana, we have to deal
with human wildlife conflict.

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:09.490
And a lot of human wildlife
conflict these days,

00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:10.943
is to do with elephants.

00:28:11.850 --> 00:28:15.040
We happen to be the home

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:20.040
of the highest number of elephants
in the wild in the world.

00:28:22.490 --> 00:28:27.090
We've looked after them on
your behalf, right.(laughs)

00:28:27.090 --> 00:28:30.155
And when I say your
behalf, I mean the world.

00:28:30.155 --> 00:28:30.988
- On behalf of humanity.

00:28:30.988 --> 00:28:33.313
- Yes, it's a resource that we all share,

00:28:34.370 --> 00:28:36.890
but we do get into conflict with them.

00:28:36.890 --> 00:28:39.420
And you've got to manage that conflict,

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:42.310
without endangering the people,

00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:45.900
without endangering the wildlife.

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:50.900
And so, it's not exactly
a transferable model,

00:28:51.350 --> 00:28:53.070
as one would wish.

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:55.220
We are a democratic system,

00:28:55.220 --> 00:28:58.950
an open liberal democratic system.

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:01.280
We've had elections every five years,

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:04.740
and it is my fervent and
hope and determination,

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:06.598
at least while, I'm in charge

00:29:06.598 --> 00:29:08.270
that they will have
elections every five years.

00:29:08.270 --> 00:29:11.430
Free, open, transparent,
and fair elections.

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:15.100
And it is the winner
who will assume power.

00:29:15.100 --> 00:29:18.730
So if we go to the elections
and I lose, I lose,

00:29:18.730 --> 00:29:23.313
I go home and I won't turn
back, I won't want to come back.

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:29.020
And so, you can't exactly impose

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:32.700
a model from a cities nation states,

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:36.193
on a developing state like Botswana.

00:29:37.180 --> 00:29:38.716
- No, thank you.

00:29:38.716 --> 00:29:40.350
Thank you for pointing out this.

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:42.790
The fact that the context
is very different,

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:46.270
and it's important, and
there are various nuances,

00:29:46.270 --> 00:29:48.950
that are unique to your nation

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:51.730
and various needs and dynamics,

00:29:51.730 --> 00:29:54.700
that make it necessary

00:29:54.700 --> 00:29:57.000
for a policy that's
responsive to those needs.

00:29:58.130 --> 00:30:01.520
We'd like to open to a few
questions from the audience.

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:02.990
I know we started a little bit late,

00:30:02.990 --> 00:30:06.200
so please raise your hand
and press the button.

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:07.900
When the light turns green,

00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:09.630
you'll be able to speak
into the microphone,

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:11.130
kindly introduce yourself,

00:30:11.130 --> 00:30:14.540
and keep it brief, because
we don't have much time.

00:30:14.540 --> 00:30:18.062
Thank you, go ahead, yeah.

00:30:18.062 --> 00:30:20.020
- [Participant] With the transition

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:22.400
to the knowledge-based
economy for Botswana,

00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:24.550
what is the Botswanian government doing

00:30:25.385 --> 00:30:28.833
to encourage entrepreneurship
among Botswanians?

00:30:30.280 --> 00:30:33.930
- One more question, gentlemen
at the bike in the gray suit,

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:35.073
the gray jacket, yep.

00:30:37.310 --> 00:30:38.750
- [James] Thank Your excellency,

00:30:38.750 --> 00:30:41.523
James Mwangi I'm a Yale
old fellow over here.

00:30:42.950 --> 00:30:47.950
You mentioned the role that
Botswana is playing as a steward

00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:49.770
and indeed many countries
on the continent playing

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:54.570
as a steward of the world's
natural biodiversity and bounty.

00:30:54.570 --> 00:30:56.600
Coming after the COVID discussions,

00:30:56.600 --> 00:30:58.507
we'll be heading into COP26,

00:30:58.507 --> 00:31:00.963
and the discussion around climate.

00:31:02.312 --> 00:31:04.080
What is your view, on what is the...

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:06.740
How is Botswana thinking
about its positioning,

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:08.340
around COP26?

00:31:08.340 --> 00:31:11.620
And what do you think is
Africa's overall role,

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:13.903
in the fight against
climate change globally?

00:31:21.213 --> 00:31:22.703
- (indistinct).

00:31:22.703 --> 00:31:25.030
All right, thank you very much.

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:26.240
What's Botswana government doing

00:31:26.240 --> 00:31:29.650
to encourage entrepreneurship in Botswana.

00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:34.650
We're providing a lot of
education and opportunity.

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:39.943
Legislative space is created,

00:31:41.140 --> 00:31:44.440
so that there are some reservations.

00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:46.890
We tussled back and forth about

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:51.890
the prudence of reservations
and the lack of it.

00:31:56.747 --> 00:31:58.660
So we're very careful in its use,

00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:01.473
but when we have infant
industries develop,

00:32:02.507 --> 00:32:06.370
and we have a nice, an
entrepreneurial culture emerge,

00:32:06.370 --> 00:32:07.720
we attempt to protect that.

00:32:09.070 --> 00:32:11.793
We provide training,
we provide mentorship,

00:32:12.850 --> 00:32:16.187
we provide procurement categories

00:32:22.067 --> 00:32:26.817
for certain things to be bought
from those entrepreneurs,

00:32:27.670 --> 00:32:29.193
but it's just never enough.

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:31.890
It's never enough because,

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:35.770
we need such a small scale
entrepreneurs to grow,

00:32:35.770 --> 00:32:37.430
and get to the next stage,

00:32:37.430 --> 00:32:39.880
until they become fully
fledged business persons.

00:32:41.310 --> 00:32:45.700
So, it's an ongoing challenge.

00:32:45.700 --> 00:32:48.463
And we do provide training
right after university.

00:32:49.780 --> 00:32:53.593
It's the relevance of that
would, which is important.

00:32:55.120 --> 00:32:58.060
James Mwangi, you asked

00:32:58.060 --> 00:33:03.060
if Botswana being a steward
of our national resources.

00:33:03.388 --> 00:33:06.650
What are we thinking about the upcoming

00:33:06.650 --> 00:33:09.723
environmental conference in
November in Edinburgh is it?

00:33:11.050 --> 00:33:12.470
- Glasgow--
- Glasgow, sorry,

00:33:12.470 --> 00:33:13.503
in Scotland.

00:33:19.014 --> 00:33:21.697
We're signatories to the Paris Accord one,

00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:28.603
we are very determined to make
sure that we play our part,

00:33:30.154 --> 00:33:34.607
in contribute into the
management of climate change.

00:33:35.530 --> 00:33:40.530
We're extremely worried, by United States,

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:43.910
whose impact is significant

00:33:44.830 --> 00:33:47.883
with respect to issues of climate change.

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:52.320
Delighted that America is back

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:54.523
as a party to the Paris Agreement.

00:33:55.899 --> 00:33:57.817
You can use your hand a
little bit. (applauding)

00:33:57.817 --> 00:33:58.650
- Oh yeah.

00:33:59.510 --> 00:34:00.970
- But you need to catch up, (laughs)

00:34:00.970 --> 00:34:02.870
you need to make up for the lost time.

00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:05.923
You really need to, because,

00:34:07.418 --> 00:34:09.243
you're such a big
economy and a big player.

00:34:10.840 --> 00:34:15.840
We need to harness the green energy space.

00:34:17.410 --> 00:34:20.520
And we may be just as guilty because,

00:34:20.520 --> 00:34:24.980
we apparently have some of
the best sun opportunity.

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:29.220
And we have a integrated
resource plan that we've approved

00:34:29.220 --> 00:34:32.563
and we're tilting to go green.

00:34:34.660 --> 00:34:37.023
We say this and we want this,

00:34:38.430 --> 00:34:41.310
in a situation where we had discovered

00:34:42.410 --> 00:34:44.173
huge deposits of coal.

00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:47.250
I mean, more than 200 billion tons.

00:34:47.250 --> 00:34:48.083
- My goodness.

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:50.680
- Can you imagine?
- Yeah.

00:34:50.680 --> 00:34:54.270
- And so, we're tussling

00:34:54.270 --> 00:34:58.853
and grappling with what
we do with all that.

00:35:00.260 --> 00:35:02.710
There were others who were
wanting to exploit it,

00:35:03.800 --> 00:35:05.593
but increasingly as you know,

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:09.085
financial institutions are not be willing

00:35:09.085 --> 00:35:10.610
to fund that beyond 2030.

00:35:10.610 --> 00:35:12.800
And so, if anything started now,

00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:15.720
it really would make a
return on investment.

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:17.473
So, so we are deeply committed.

00:35:18.380 --> 00:35:20.450
We want other countries in Africa,

00:35:20.450 --> 00:35:22.340
at least those we associated with,

00:35:22.340 --> 00:35:25.810
in the national economy management space,

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:28.939
to go together with us on that,

00:35:28.939 --> 00:35:30.160
but it needs huge investment.

00:35:30.160 --> 00:35:32.920
And a lot of investment is R&amp;D.

00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:36.200
And so we look to learning,
leading researchers,

00:35:36.200 --> 00:35:39.490
institutions such as Yale,

00:35:39.490 --> 00:35:42.460
to help us at least with the capacitation,

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:45.533
to be able to help us else in on that.

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:50.613
- Well, they don't envy
you having to deal with

00:35:50.613 --> 00:35:52.280
that decision about the
coal deposits, (laughs)

00:35:52.280 --> 00:35:57.280
but last I checked diamonds
come from carbon and coal.

00:35:57.728 --> 00:35:59.958
So this could bode well for the future

00:35:59.958 --> 00:36:01.330
of Botswana in some way.
- Yeah. (giggles)

00:36:01.330 --> 00:36:03.030
- We'll take a few more questions.

00:36:04.416 --> 00:36:08.313
One here, this gentleman,
and a lot of hand back there,

00:36:09.280 --> 00:36:13.060
orange shirt next, yes.

00:36:13.060 --> 00:36:17.440
- [Desmond] My name is
Desmond and I have a question

00:36:18.520 --> 00:36:22.100
regarding trans boundary relationship

00:36:22.100 --> 00:36:24.340
between Botswana and her neighbors.

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:28.170
And how you intend, or your approach,

00:36:28.170 --> 00:36:30.110
towards ensuring that these initiative,

00:36:30.110 --> 00:36:32.060
that you're undertaking,

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:35.380
is also transient and it moves

00:36:35.380 --> 00:36:37.920
into this other, your counterpart,

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:41.930
and that you become an influential point

00:36:41.930 --> 00:36:45.310
where all the other
countries can look around you

00:36:45.310 --> 00:36:47.550
and begin to copy what you're doing.

00:36:47.550 --> 00:36:49.590
Is there initiative to do that?

00:36:49.590 --> 00:36:52.390
Or are you just focusing
more on the protection needs

00:36:53.791 --> 00:36:55.163
than just Botswana itself as a country?

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:00.273
- Yes.

00:37:02.590 --> 00:37:05.089
- [Abraham] Thank you Your Excellency,

00:37:05.089 --> 00:37:07.000
my name is Abraham Kaita
and I'm from Liberia.

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:08.820
A student in Yale College.

00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:11.326
First of all, I wanted to say thank you

00:37:11.326 --> 00:37:12.605
to the people of Botswana

00:37:12.605 --> 00:37:14.530
for being the shining city on a hill

00:37:14.530 --> 00:37:17.863
for prioritizing democracy in Africa.

00:37:17.863 --> 00:37:22.863
My question has to do with,
what is your government doing

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:25.260
to promote women empowerment,

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:28.150
and women involved in
democratic processes?

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:30.850
Oh, yes, we celebrate Botswana

00:37:30.850 --> 00:37:35.356
for having a sustained
decades of democratic rule.

00:37:35.356 --> 00:37:38.610
We have Seretse Khama, Ketumile Masire,

00:37:41.660 --> 00:37:43.727
and all the four presidents before you.

00:37:43.727 --> 00:37:45.419
But what is the government doing

00:37:45.419 --> 00:37:47.441
to ensure that women are inclusive

00:37:47.441 --> 00:37:49.181
in the governing process of Botswana?

00:37:49.181 --> 00:37:50.014
Thank you.

00:37:51.809 --> 00:37:54.680
- Just one more, all right.

00:37:54.680 --> 00:37:56.110
- Hi my name is Abby Coke

00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:57.950
I'm from the School of Public Health.

00:37:57.950 --> 00:38:00.480
Thank you so much for
coming to speak to us today.

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:02.130
And I wanted to ask,

00:38:02.130 --> 00:38:03.770
what does cooperation look like

00:38:03.770 --> 00:38:06.190
within the continent for
you, and for Botswana,

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:09.163
under this lens of
knowledge-based economy?

00:38:10.784 --> 00:38:11.617
- Sorry, could you ask again?

00:38:11.617 --> 00:38:12.966
What is?

00:38:12.966 --> 00:38:13.799
- (indistinct).

00:38:13.799 --> 00:38:14.632
- Can you say it again, pleas.

00:38:14.632 --> 00:38:16.010
- Oh, what does cooperation look like

00:38:16.010 --> 00:38:18.060
on the continent of Africa

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:20.720
under this lens of
knowledge-based economy?

00:38:29.718 --> 00:38:30.738
- Take one more, yeah.

00:38:30.738 --> 00:38:31.571
- One more.
- One more.

00:38:31.571 --> 00:38:32.404
- Let's do it, Udu.

00:38:36.544 --> 00:38:41.544
- [Udu] Yeah, my name
is Ugu Udu from Nigeria.

00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:50.633
So I was, I'm really curious to know,

00:38:51.470 --> 00:38:53.890
just like the last person said,

00:38:53.890 --> 00:38:56.930
Botswana is one of the shining lights

00:38:56.930 --> 00:39:00.360
on democracy in the continent.

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:03.110
But if you look at current reports,

00:39:03.110 --> 00:39:07.053
Freedom House Economic Intelligence,

00:39:08.140 --> 00:39:12.670
decline of democracy in the
continent is really worrying.

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:16.340
Increasingly a lot of
countries in Africa are going

00:39:17.545 --> 00:39:22.545
from democracy to hybrid
democracy and plain dictatorships.

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:27.470
And the African Union has
one of his commitments,

00:39:27.470 --> 00:39:29.980
the protection of
democracy in the continent,

00:39:29.980 --> 00:39:34.730
and serious resistance to
undemocratic change of power.

00:39:34.730 --> 00:39:38.840
I just wanted to know the
Council of the Head of States,

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:41.780
what are you guys doing at this point,

00:39:41.780 --> 00:39:44.070
to address this decline?

00:39:44.070 --> 00:39:47.840
And what lessons, can
we pick from Botswana?

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:48.990
What has worked in Botswana

00:39:48.990 --> 00:39:52.239
that's not working now in
the other parts of Africa,

00:39:52.239 --> 00:39:55.640
that we're having this terrible,
terrible loss to democracy.

00:39:55.640 --> 00:39:58.040
We have about three, four
coup on the continent,

00:39:59.315 --> 00:40:00.200
between last year and this year.

00:40:00.200 --> 00:40:01.860
So what was your take on this?

00:40:01.860 --> 00:40:02.693
- Thank you.

00:40:04.810 --> 00:40:08.890
- Udu, thank you so much.(laughs)

00:40:08.890 --> 00:40:10.110
Let me begin with the...

00:40:11.937 --> 00:40:12.770
Where do we start now?

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:13.603
Was it Abraham Kaita?

00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:16.275
Were you the first one?

00:40:16.275 --> 00:40:17.108
- No,

00:40:17.108 --> 00:40:18.000
he's the first one.
- No, Desmond.

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:18.833
- Desmond.

00:40:18.833 --> 00:40:19.810
- I think it was Desmond, right?

00:40:19.810 --> 00:40:20.850
- Yes.

00:40:20.850 --> 00:40:22.850
- Botswana and her neighbors?

00:40:22.850 --> 00:40:25.733
Yes, how we influencing one another?

00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:30.673
We are part of a region,

00:40:31.830 --> 00:40:36.830
both in the economic
space, through the SACU,

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:40.680
the oldest customs union in
the world, began in 1910.

00:40:40.680 --> 00:40:44.600
That's between Botswana,
Eswatini, Lesotho,

00:40:44.600 --> 00:40:45.923
Namibia and South Africa.

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:49.673
And so, we cooperate.

00:40:51.090 --> 00:40:56.090
It's not an equitable system,
as many unions are not.

00:40:59.987 --> 00:41:03.660
And so, we were trying very
hard to make it work better,

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:05.740
as we all develop.

00:41:05.740 --> 00:41:07.383
We also part of SADCC,

00:41:08.400 --> 00:41:11.563
that's a social economic
and political organization.

00:41:12.930 --> 00:41:17.913
And it's got a big
number of member states.

00:41:19.910 --> 00:41:24.910
Where in, we also have a very
political and focused organ,

00:41:27.560 --> 00:41:30.620
which I just happily chaired for a year.

00:41:30.620 --> 00:41:33.224
And I had to over to President Ramaphosa.

00:41:33.224 --> 00:41:36.530
The Organ Politics Defense
and Security Cooperation.

00:41:36.530 --> 00:41:40.650
It is that instrument, more
than any other within SADCC,

00:41:40.650 --> 00:41:44.650
that is used to influence one another

00:41:44.650 --> 00:41:49.650
in the political space, between countries,

00:41:50.330 --> 00:41:54.210
in the spirit of promoting
political cooperation,

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:57.463
promote defense cooperation,
security cooperation.

00:41:59.337 --> 00:42:03.010
And a battery of values

00:42:03.010 --> 00:42:06.390
for holding elections, for
instance, is generated.

00:42:06.390 --> 00:42:10.200
A system of monitoring for
elections is generated,

00:42:10.200 --> 00:42:14.080
a system of presidential
influencing is in...

00:42:14.080 --> 00:42:16.079
And I could just let you know,

00:42:16.079 --> 00:42:17.590
I wish I had my phone here to take it out.

00:42:17.590 --> 00:42:22.590
I was messaging up in between
ourselves and President Lungu

00:42:24.060 --> 00:42:26.440
as the elections were unfolding.

00:42:26.440 --> 00:42:29.307
And part of that messaging
was to she to ask him,

00:42:29.307 --> 00:42:33.560
"Hey, let's be with it.

00:42:33.560 --> 00:42:38.560
I mean, it doesn't look
like you've made it.

00:42:38.590 --> 00:42:41.337
So please accept the results."

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:47.360
It's not easy to say that to
another head of state because,

00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:52.173
we often restricted by the
considerations of sovereignty.

00:42:54.290 --> 00:42:57.160
But we as colleagues,

00:42:57.160 --> 00:43:00.770
you'll find that when
you're president. (laughs)

00:43:00.770 --> 00:43:03.600
You talk to one another
sometimes very frankly,

00:43:03.600 --> 00:43:06.370
particularly when you
develop a friendship.

00:43:06.370 --> 00:43:08.470
And so, we've developed that within SADCC.

00:43:09.640 --> 00:43:13.340
And it doesn't always work, right.

00:43:13.340 --> 00:43:15.770
I must admit it doesn't always work,

00:43:15.770 --> 00:43:19.590
because you can only do
and say things to a point.

00:43:19.590 --> 00:43:21.520
And it's only when it gets really bad,

00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:23.543
that you, you would raise alarm.

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:27.090
There's also the...

00:43:27.090 --> 00:43:29.090
And this is a continental wide one.

00:43:29.090 --> 00:43:32.393
It gets to part of what
Udu, I think is asking.

00:43:33.830 --> 00:43:35.410
It gets a little more complicated

00:43:35.410 --> 00:43:38.450
when it gets to the
African Union and bigger.

00:43:38.450 --> 00:43:41.950
Many of African countries,
including those in the south,

00:43:41.950 --> 00:43:45.290
attained the independence
through struggle, right.

00:43:45.290 --> 00:43:47.070
They fought for liberation.

00:43:47.070 --> 00:43:49.210
And when you're a liberation hero,

00:43:49.210 --> 00:43:54.210
that seems to cling on, and
it seems to blind some people

00:43:56.310 --> 00:44:01.310
into not seeing on pointing
out, not accepting,

00:44:01.690 --> 00:44:03.470
some things that may be going wrong

00:44:03.470 --> 00:44:05.233
that may be happening.

00:44:06.810 --> 00:44:10.450
And there's a lot of solidarity that

00:44:12.592 --> 00:44:15.720
has being part of the
narrative for a very long time.

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:19.430
But that's slowly changing
with the generations,

00:44:19.430 --> 00:44:20.790
that come on.

00:44:20.790 --> 00:44:25.523
But, outright condemnation,

00:44:26.685 --> 00:44:31.685
and is often viewed very
negatively and not effective.

00:44:32.580 --> 00:44:37.580
It's best to engage as countries relate.

00:44:38.860 --> 00:44:41.677
So yes, I would accept what you say Udu,

00:44:43.800 --> 00:44:47.473
democracy, it would
seem is on the decline.

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:50.150
And it's very worrying.

00:44:52.880 --> 00:44:55.420
There are a number of
coups have taken place.

00:44:55.420 --> 00:45:00.333
There was a time when
the AU abhorred coups,

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:04.140
but we still do...

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:07.393
But they still do happen.

00:45:08.790 --> 00:45:12.520
So, there's a lot more than
a thing needs to be done,

00:45:12.520 --> 00:45:16.310
in terms of capacitating and training,

00:45:16.310 --> 00:45:20.160
those who would manage
their affairs in Africa,

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:22.343
there's not enough of it being done.

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:27.860
Abraham Kaita, we thank you...

00:45:27.860 --> 00:45:30.290
So you thank us for democracy in Africa,

00:45:30.290 --> 00:45:32.498
but what are we doing in Botswana

00:45:32.498 --> 00:45:35.780
to promote women empowerment in politics?

00:45:35.780 --> 00:45:39.310
Gosh, I wish the women would
have answered that. (laughs)

00:45:39.310 --> 00:45:43.750
We do everything we can
to try and encourage them.

00:45:43.750 --> 00:45:46.510
And I speak on behalf
of even the opposition,

00:45:46.510 --> 00:45:49.793
the political parties in
Botswana, we do very badly.

00:45:51.560 --> 00:45:54.380
Many women have said very openly,

00:45:54.380 --> 00:45:57.083
they find politics in
Botswana unattractive,

00:45:57.960 --> 00:45:59.283
it's vicious and nasty.

00:46:00.640 --> 00:46:05.640
And so, there's also the aspect of costs.

00:46:07.330 --> 00:46:12.330
Over time, men have had great opportunity

00:46:12.960 --> 00:46:16.490
to generate income more
than women generally.

00:46:16.490 --> 00:46:18.370
But what we have done as a government,

00:46:18.370 --> 00:46:21.543
the executive level, is
promoted a lot of women.

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:25.743
Probably 50% of my permanent
secretaries are women.

00:46:26.670 --> 00:46:29.269
At least, all the permanent secretaries,

00:46:29.269 --> 00:46:30.230
from the permanent
secretary to the president,

00:46:30.230 --> 00:46:32.630
the deputy permanent
secretary to the president,

00:46:33.493 --> 00:46:35.470
and all the permanent
secretaries in the presidency,

00:46:35.470 --> 00:46:38.370
three ministries of the
presidency in Botswana, are women.

00:46:39.600 --> 00:46:41.338
And one of them is here,

00:46:41.338 --> 00:46:44.602
permanent secretary from ministry
of international affairs.

00:46:44.602 --> 00:46:45.710
(applauding)

00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:46.543
There're women.

00:46:47.640 --> 00:46:48.810
But you can have a conversation with her,

00:46:48.810 --> 00:46:53.570
why she doesn't skip over and
contest elections. (laughs)

00:46:53.570 --> 00:46:55.953
And others as my chief of protocol,

00:46:56.820 --> 00:46:59.120
that's a woman, deputy
permanent secretary level,

00:46:59.120 --> 00:47:00.420
you wanna raise your hand.

